  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "A coin is being randomly picked from a jar and put back in until getting a one-dollar coin. The jar holds a $70%$ of one-dollar coins. What is the probability that a one-dollar coin is chosen before the $5$th trial?  Round your answer to $3$ decimal places.",
  "worked_solution": "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of choices before choosing a one-dollar coin. Since the chance of choosing a one-dollar coin is $70%,$ the number of choices required to choose a one-dollar coin can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=70%=\\frac{70}{100}=0.7.\n$$\n\nSo, $X\u223c\\text{Geom}(0.7).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.7)^{x-1}(0.7) \\\\  & =(0.3)^{x-1}(0.7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of choosing a one-dollar coin before the $5$th trial is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<5) & =P(X\\in {1,2,3,4}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3)+f(4) \\\\  & =(0.3)^{1-1}(0.7)+(0.3)^{2-1}(0.7)+(0.3)^{3-1}(0.7)+(0.3)^{4-1}(0.7) \\\\  & =(0.7)+(0.3)(0.7)+(0.3)^{2}(0.7)+(0.3)^{3}(0.7) \\\\  & =[1+0.3+(0.3)^{2}+(0.3)^{3}](0.7) \\\\  & \u22480.992\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.",
